{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/443795-resistance-and-social-skills-in-2e","id":443795,"title":"Resistance and Social Skills in 2e","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-03-15T23:15:58-07:00","id":443795,"title":"Resistance and Social Skills in 2e","content":"Okay, so... I've tried to just work around it, but this little contradiction is driving me batty.<br /> <br /> The resistance section in 2e says that you should subtract a trait if the number of successes matters, like combat and defense. Got it.<br /> <br /> It also says you roll contested if only success/failure really matters, like supernatural powers. Got it.<br /> <br /> But then it goes on to list a series of examples. The social rolls given by way of example all (except one, which is not resisted) use Resistance rather than a Contested Roll.<br /> <br /> Is there a rule somewhere that explains how social rolls are graded on number of successes? I mean, there are some powers and a lot of conditions that give bonuses to resisting contested social rolls, so one would infer that it's to be expected that you use Contested for social rolls.<br /> <br /> Is it in Social Maneuvering? It didn't seem to be the last few times I've read it. Can someone please explain? If it's indeed meant to be Contested (everywhere except those examples suggests that it is), what do you pair with resolve/composure, typically?<br /> <br /> At the risk of derailing my primary question with a secondary one (has happened to me before), how would you handle Social Maneuvering if PCs need to convince someone -right now,- and not several weeks away? That's probably my biggest issue. It makes no thematic sense to just wander off for a while then come back to do another favor and get another +1 to convincing. I love social combat in games, just... Social Maneuvering doesn't make any thematic sense to me, even if I understand the mechanics.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1692-maina","name":"Maina","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1692_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"724"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-15T23:39:29-07:00","id":443824,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Maina</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/443795-resistance-and-social-skills-in-2e#post443795\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">At the risk of derailing my primary question with a secondary one (has happened to me before), how would you handle Social Maneuvering if PCs need to convince someone -right now,- and not several weeks away? That's probably my biggest issue. It makes no thematic sense to just wander off for a while then come back to do another favor and get another +1 to convincing. I love social combat in games, just... Social Maneuvering doesn't make any thematic sense to me, even if I understand the mechanics.</div> </div> </div> </div>When you're trying to get someone to do something for you right now, you Force Doors.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-16T03:41:13-07:00","id":443986,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Maina</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/443795-resistance-and-social-skills-in-2e#post443795\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Is there a rule somewhere that explains how social rolls are graded on number of successes? I mean, there are some powers and a lot of conditions that give bonuses to resisting contested social rolls, so one would infer that it's to be expected that you use Contested for social rolls.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think those examples are meant for ease of play in less critical situations. The only reason they are not contested is because they assume minor NPCs, at least that is my (very interpreting) reading of them. When dealing with important NPCs, you make it properly contested.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Maina</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/443795-resistance-and-social-skills-in-2e#post443795\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">At the risk of derailing my primary question with a secondary one (has happened to me before), how would you handle Social Maneuvering if PCs need to convince someone -right now,- and not several weeks away? That's probably my biggest issue. It makes no thematic sense to just wander off for a while then come back to do another favor and get another +1 to convincing. I love social combat in games, just... Social Maneuvering doesn't make any thematic sense to me, even if I understand the mechanics.</div> </div> </div> </div>As Leetsepeak says, that is what Forcing Doors is for. Opening doors, one at a time, is the smooth approach. The lobbyist approach. It is getting someone to do something for you without the risk of destroying relations over it. If you need something right the eff now, you Force Doors. Keep in mind that Forcing Doors does not automatically include Hard Leverage.<br /> <br /> Do note though, that with a good circumstantial First Impression and a successful First Impression roll, you are already at the one-hour-per-roll interval, which should usually be enough.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Politeness is the lubricant of social intercourse.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/272-saibot","name":"saibot","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar272_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1063"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-16T14:56:36-07:00","id":444930,"title":"","content":"Thank you. I forgot about Forcing Doors. Just seemed really risky.<br /> <br /> If one is expected to use resistance only for minor NPCs, that still leaves me with the question: What do you pair with Resolve or Composure to contest social rolls? I mean, say you're trying to convince the Prince not to ash your buddy for being an idiot neonate, and say the ST isn't using Social Maneuvering. Or maybe the ST is, and they want it to be contested (since after re-reading it I read that's sometimes an option). I would assume resolve + ???. Not composure, if it's a rational argument. Empathy? Persuasion?<br /> <br /> Once again, thanks for the responses so far.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1692-maina","name":"Maina","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1692_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"724"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-16T16:28:28-07:00","id":445060,"title":"","content":"That really depends on the situation and what skills the player is using in this example. Empathy, Subterfuge and Persuasion are usually good skills to use. Otherwise use Resolve + Composure as the ol' fallback. I think your examples fits this. In some cases it might even use Mental Skills like Politics. Often Wits or Intelligence might play into Resistance as well (like discerning if the thing someone tries to talk you into is actually a good idea for you might be Intelligence + Resolve).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Politeness is the lubricant of social intercourse.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/272-saibot","name":"saibot","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar272_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1063"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-16T16:30:06-07:00","id":445066,"title":"","content":"Usually it's Resolve/Composure + Supernatural Tolerance.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-16T17:04:53-07:00","id":445087,"title":"","content":"Supernatural tolerance on a mundane roll? And thanks again. I probably sounded grumpy in the OP, so I appreciate the responses.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1692-maina","name":"Maina","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1692_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"724"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-16T18:39:27-07:00","id":445158,"title":"","content":"Oh, my bad. For mundane rolls, I'd say Saibot's advice is right on.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}